module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Maps
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.TensorProduct.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.SquareZeroConormal
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# Flat base change of the conormal module of an ideal

Let `R → S` be a commutative algebra, let `I` be an ideal of `R` and let `I S` denote the ideal
`Ideal.map (algebraMap R S) I` of `S` generated by the image of `I`.  Multiplying inside `S`
sends a class `x + I ^ 2` with `x ∈ I` and a scalar `b : S` to the class of `b * x` in
`I S ⧸ (I S) ^ 2`, giving a canonical `R`-linear map

`I ⧸ I ^ 2 ⊗[R] S ⟶ I S ⧸ (I S) ^ 2`,

that is `Ideal.cotangentBaseChange`.  This file proves that it is **always surjective** and that
it is an **isomorphism as soon as `S` is flat over `R`**.

This is the commutative algebra content of the flat half of [Stacks Project, Tag 0473], whose
proof reads in full: "the natural map `I'/(I')² ⊗_{R'} R → I/I²` is surjective; in the flat case
one has `I = I' ⊗_{R'} R` and `I² = (I')² ⊗_{R'} R`, from which it follows that the map is an
isomorphism".  Here `R'` is the base and `R` the flat algebra, so the roles of the letters are
`R' = R` and `R = S`.  The two identifications the source invokes are `Ideal.tensorEquivMap`,
which is `I S ≃ I ⊗[R] S` for `S` flat, and that same statement applied to `I ^ 2` together with
`Ideal.map_pow`, which gives `(I S) ^ 2 = I ^ 2 ⊗[R] S`.

Both halves are proved through `Ideal.cotangentToQuotientSquare`, the injection of `I ⧸ I ^ 2`
into `S ⧸ (I S) ^ 2`: composing with it turns the canonical map into multiplication in `S`
followed by a ring quotient, which is `Ideal.tensorSMulHom` of
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.SquareZeroConormal` read modulo `(I S) ^ 2`.  That single
computation, `Ideal.cotangentToQuotientSquare_cotangentBaseChange_rTensor`, carries both
directions: surjectivity because the image of `Ideal.tensorSMulHom` is all of `I S`, and
injectivity because flatness makes `Ideal.tensorSMulHom` injective, so an element of `I ⊗[R] S`
whose product lies in `(I S) ^ 2 = I ^ 2 ⊗[R] S` already comes from `I ^ 2 ⊗[R] S` and therefore
dies in `I ⧸ I ^ 2 ⊗[R] S`.

## Main definitions

* `Ideal.algebraMapRestrict`: the structure map `R → S` restricted to `I → I S`.
* `Ideal.toMapCotangent`: the composite `I → I S → I S ⧸ (I S) ^ 2`.
* `Ideal.mapCotangentHom`: its factorisation `I ⧸ I ^ 2 → I S ⧸ (I S) ^ 2`, well defined because
  the image of `I · I` lands in `(I S) ^ 2`.
* `Ideal.cotangentBaseChange`: **the canonical map** `I ⧸ I ^ 2 ⊗[R] S → I S ⧸ (I S) ^ 2`.
* `Ideal.cotangentBaseChangeEquiv`: the canonical map as an isomorphism, for `S` flat over `R`.
* `Ideal.tensorSMulHomToMap` and `Ideal.tensorEquivMap`: multiplication `I ⊗[R] S → I S`, and the
  isomorphism it is for `S` flat over `R` — the source's `I = I' ⊗_{R'} R`.

## Main results

* `Ideal.bijective_cotangentBaseChange`: **the flat case of [Stacks Project, Tag 0473] in the
  affine picture.** For `S` flat over `R` the canonical map
  `I ⧸ I ^ 2 ⊗[R] S → I S ⧸ (I S) ^ 2` is bijective.
* `Ideal.surjective_cotangentBaseChange`: the canonical map is surjective, with **no flatness
  hypothesis** — the affine picture of the surjectivity half of the same tag, which is proved for
  sheaves of modules by `Stacks.Immersion.epi_immersionConormalSheafMap`.
* `Ideal.injective_cotangentBaseChange`: the canonical map is injective for `S` flat over `R`.
* `Ideal.bijective_tensorSMulHomToMap`: for `S` flat over `R` multiplication `I ⊗[R] S → I S` is
  bijective.
* `Ideal.cotangentToQuotientSquare_cotangentBaseChange_rTensor`: the computation both halves run
  on.
* `Ideal.mem_map_algebraMap_iff_exists_tensorSMulHom`: an element of `S` lies in `I S` exactly
  when it is a product coming from `I ⊗[R] S`.

## Implementation notes

The target of the canonical map is `Ideal.Cotangent` of the extended ideal, and the source is a
tensor product over `R` rather than over `R ⧸ I`.  The latter is the form the source statement
uses, and it is the one in which the flatness criteria apply; it agrees with the pullback form
`I ⧸ I ^ 2 ⊗[R ⧸ I] (S ⧸ I S)` because `I` annihilates `I ⧸ I ^ 2` and `S ⧸ I S` is
`R ⧸ I ⊗[R] S`, but **that comparison is not proved here** (see the partial coverage below).

Nothing in the statement or the proof needs `I ^ 2 = 0`; this is the general-ideal statement, of
which `Ideal.bijective_tensorSMulHomToKer_iff` of
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.SquareZeroConormal` is the square-zero relative, and the two are
independent: that one characterises flatness of `S` over `R` by the canonical map being
invertible, this one deduces invertibility from flatness and needs no first-order thickening.

Surjectivity is proved first and separately, because it holds with no hypothesis at all, so that
the flat half is exactly the injectivity statement.

`Ideal.tensorSMulHom`, `Ideal.range_tensorSMulHom` and `Ideal.injective_tensorSMulHom_iff` are
reused from `StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.SquareZeroConormal` instead of being restated; this is
why `R` and `S` are constrained to the same universe, which is the universe discipline of that
file.  No mathematical hypothesis is hidden in it: every ring in the intended geometric
application is a ring of functions on a scheme, hence in the scheme's universe.

The maps out of a subtype are built with `LinearMap.restrict` and `LinearMap.codRestrict` into
`Submodule.restrictScalars R (I.map (algebraMap R S))`, whose coercion to a type is the coercion
of the ideal itself, so the composites with the `S`-linear `Ideal.toCotangent` typecheck after
`LinearMap.restrictScalars`.

## Partial coverage

What is proved here is the **affine, commutative algebra** statement.  The sheaf-level statement
of [Stacks Project, Tag 0473] for a cartesian square of immersions of schemes

```
Z --i--→ X
|        |
f        g
↓        ↓
Z' -i'-→ X'
```

with `g` flat — that `Stacks.Immersion.immersionConormalSheafMap` is an isomorphism — is **not**
proved here, and neither is any geometric statement.  The surjectivity half at that level is
`Stacks.Immersion.epi_immersionConormalSheafMap` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Conormal.SquareEpi`, which tests surjectivity on affine charts of
the square through the adjunction, so it never has to compute the sections of the pullback sheaf
`f^* 𝒞_{Z'/X'}`.  Invertibility cannot be tested that way: it needs those sections, or the
stalks, identified with a tensor product, and that identification is not available over an open
(`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.RingedSpace.ModulePullbackStalk` has it for stalks only).  Nothing
in this file is therefore an input to [Stacks Project, Tag 04CQ] or [Stacks Project, Tag 06BH]
yet; the missing step between them is that globalisation.

